    "created_at": "2024-08-14T17:50:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-08-14T17:50:40Z",
    "body": "I think you're asking what prevents AI features that consume web content from simply ignoring this attribute, did I understand correctly?\r\n\r\nThis is in a similar vein as robots.txt in that it can be ignored, but for a well meaning implementation that tries to do \"the right thing\", it's a useful hint about the sensitivity of the contents. \r\n\r\nWhen an AI model consumes web contents, and then communicates with some other unrelated entity for example, the sensitive information leak from the web contents to the entity is frequently unintentional by the AI model/tool author. This property would provide a strong hint that the contents should be avoided.\r\n\r\nI admit that this is a bit of a cart-before-the-horse in that the prevalence of tools that consume web contents in an AI context is currently low. It is also not necessarily something that existing browser implementations need to worry about. However, if AI features _are_ going to be added to browsers, then this may become more important.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
